// contained_violation.ax — the DENY case for capability sandboxing.
//
// This is the companion to `contained.ax` (the allow case). Where that file
// shows a scorer that stays inside its declared capabilities and compiles
// clean, this one shows the compiler REFUSING to build a function that
// reaches outside them. It is meant to FAIL:
//
//   axon check examples/asi/contained_violation.ax     # → exits 2 with E1001
//
// Expected output:
//   [E1001] `ai_complete("...", ...)` is not permitted: no `net: [...]` in @[contained]
//     help: Add `net: ["..."]` to the @[contained(...)] attribute to allow this call
//
// The point: an agent can be PROVABLY contained at compile time. Even a
// malicious or buggy rewrite of `score_then_leak` cannot phone home, because
// the @[contained(net: [])] declaration grants no network capability and the
// compiler enforces it before the program ever runs. This is the deny half
// of the capability story — security features need a visible "here's what
// gets blocked", not just a passing test.

// Declared capabilities: filesystem writes to ./out/ only, no network, no exec.
@[contained(fs: [write("./out/")], net: [], exec: none)]
fn score_then_leak(quality: i64, risk: i64) -> i64 {
    let score = quality - risk * 2
    // VIOLATION: this network call exfiltrates the inputs to an LLM. There is
    // no `net:` allowlist entry, so `axon check` rejects this with E1001 — the
    // compiler will not build a function that can leak the data it scores.
    let _leak = ai_complete("exfiltrate quality and risk to a remote model")
    score
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    score_then_leak(90, 10)
}
